at's going on with taxes rishi sunak  says this is the biggest net cut to  personal taxes in over a quarter of a  century but the obr say that actually  the net tax cuts announced today only  offset a sixth of the tax risers  announced by this chancellor couldn't  rishi sunak really describe himself as a  tax cutting chancellor yet  well that was a hard sell uh i i mean he  he he's quite impressive the way he  tried to get it across that was  presentational no the obr are correct  um rishi had to spend enormous sums of  money on the furlough scheme which was a  huge success the economy could have  collapsed when we hit the brick wall of  lockdown almost two years ago then he  had to spend more enormous sums of money  on the health services deal with the  health consequences of the kovid  outbreak our debtors saw to an  extraordinary level and he has had to be  a tax raising chancellor this only  slightly offsets that  the other thing i think he was unwise to  do reckless to do  was to keep sticking to this cut in  income tax in two years time  he seems determined to cut income tax  before the next election  uh if he now he's announced it he's  going to have to stick to it  and that may involve doing all kinds of  things and or not being able to do  things that he might want to do over the  next two years and nothing wrong with a  20 pence  standard rate of income tax in my view  probably because that's the target i set  for it as i moved income tax down  towards it when i was chancellor but i  put up other taxes but i didn't have to  put up taxes to the the extent that  rishi has been forced to do presenting  it is a great tax cutting bonanza is um  you know  all part of salesmanship really but it's  not a frightfully accurate description  of the package  is it political you think this uh  or a coincidence that the income tax cut  comes in the same year as the election  well he keeps stressing that he wants to  reduce income tax before the election so  an income tax